# 7. Arbitrary free reversible switching is easy away from the stationary boundary

Now start directly with a finite family

\[
S_1,\ldots,S_k
\]

of strictly-positive symmetric stochastic matrices on \(m\) states.

Because each is symmetric and stochastic:

- uniform \(u\) is stationary;
- \(\mathbf1^\perp\) is invariant.

Since each \(S_i\) is positive and primitive, every nonstationary eigenvalue satisfies

\[
|\lambda|<1.
\]

Set

\[
\boxed{
\rho
=
\max_i
\left\|
S_i|_{\mathbf1^\perp}
\right\|_2
<1.
}
\]

For an arbitrary free word \(w\),

\[
\left\|
S_w|_{\mathbf1^\perp}
\right\|_2
\le
\rho^{|w|}.
\]

For any initial distribution \(\mu\) and observable \(f\), write

\[
\bar f=uf.
\]

Then:

### Theorem CW-002N.4 — Free-switching stationary envelope

\[
\boxed{
|\mu S_wf-\bar f|
\le
\rho^{|w|}
\,
\|\mu-u\|_2
\,
\|f-\bar f\mathbf1\|_2.
}
\]

Thus any threshold

\[
\lambda\ne\bar f
\]

has a computable finite search horizon.

So even for unrestricted noncommuting reversible switching:

\[
\boxed{
\text{all possible hard behavior is forced onto zero margin}.
}
\]

This extends the CW-002H stationary-margin principle to arbitrary free products of positive symmetric kernels.

