# 8. Local self-adjointness does not preserve real spectrum under products

One might hope that every symmetric step having real spectrum prevents the phase arithmetic responsible for nonreversible hardness.

That is false.

For two positive-definite symmetric matrices \(A,B\),

\[
AB
\]

has positive real eigenvalues: it is similar to

\[
B^{1/2}AB^{1/2}.
\]

But three positive-definite factors can already have nonreal eigenvalues.

The general positive-definite product literature confirms that the three-factor regime is qualitatively richer than the two-factor regime.

CW-002N gives a fully stochastic rational witness.

