# 9. Exact four-state positive-definite reversible witness

Start with three rational symmetric positive-definite \(3\times3\) tangent matrices:

\[
A_1
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1/2&1/4&0\\
1/4&1/2&0\\
0&0&1/5
\end{pmatrix},
\]

\[
A_2
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1/5&1/10&0\\
1/10&4/5&0\\
0&0&1/5
\end{pmatrix},
\]

\[
A_3
=
\begin{pmatrix}
2/5&-3/20&0\\
-3/20&1/10&0\\
0&0&1/5
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Each is positive definite.

Their product has eigenvalues

\[
\boxed{
\frac1{50}\pm
i\frac{\sqrt{59}}{800},
\qquad
\frac1{125}.
}
\]

Embed with the order-\(4\) Sylvester Hadamard matrix and choose

\[
\varepsilon=\frac12.
\]

This gives three \(4\times4\) matrices \(S_1,S_2,S_3\), each:

- rational;
- entrywise strictly positive;
- symmetric;
- doubly stochastic;
- positive definite.

The product has spectrum

\[
\boxed{
1,\quad
\frac1{1000},
\quad
\frac1{400}
\pm
i\frac{\sqrt{59}}{6400}.
}
\]

Therefore:

### Theorem CW-002N.5 — Three-step phase resurrection

There exist three rational strictly-positive positive-definite symmetric stochastic matrices whose threefold product has nonreal spectrum.

Since a product of two positive-definite symmetric matrices has only positive real eigenvalues, length \(3\) is the first possible product length for this phenomenon in the positive-definite class.

### Interpretation

\[
\boxed{
\text{every local step: self-adjoint and phase-free}
}
\]

does not imply

\[
\boxed{
\text{macro-dynamics: phase-free}.
}
\]

Noncommutativity manufactures complex phase from real spectral ingredients.

