# 11. Collision audit

## Hadamard and symmetric doubly-stochastic matrices

Hadamard matrices are classical tools in inverse eigenvalue problems for symmetric doubly-stochastic matrices. Constructions of the form

\[
\frac1m HDH^\top
\]

are established for realizing spectra.

The present use is different:

\[
\Phi(A)
=
\frac1m BAB^\top
\]

is exploited as a **multiplicative noncommutative algebra embedding**, not merely a diagonal spectral realization.

A direct search did not locate this exact stochastic-semigroup embedding formulation.

No novelty claim is made.

## Products of positive semidefinite/definite matrices

The fact that two positive-definite factors have positive real spectrum is classical.

The literature on products of positive semidefinite matrices explicitly studies three-, four-, and five-factor representations and shows that the three-factor class is substantially richer.

Thus "three factors can recover complex spectrum" is not itself new.

The stochastic four-state witness and its role in the reversibility computability phase diagram are the CWR-specific synthesis.

## Symmetric matrix-semigroup sign reachability

The general matrix-semigroup literature contains many undecidability results, but the exact restriction

\[
\boxed{
A_i=A_i^\top
}
\]

for scalar sign reachability was not resolved by the sources inspected in this wave.

This is now the core collision target.

